# Notification channel ids for examples/deploy-gcp/monitoring/apply.sh.
#
# Resource ids only, never tokens: a Slack channel here is the id of a
# Cloud Monitoring notification channel (authorised once in the console, or
# created through the API with a Slack bot token that can post to the
# channel; see the fleet tier below), and an email channel is created with
# `gcloud beta monitoring channels create`. The ids
# are substituted into policies/*.json where they appear as __SLACK_INFRA__
# and __EMAIL_OWNER__; apply.sh refuses to run while any of them is empty,
# because a policy with no channel is a policy nobody hears.
#
# Infra tier: a notification channel into the Slack channel your infra
# alerts go to.
SLACK_INFRA=
# Fleet tier: a notification channel into the Slack channel where the
# per-laptop alerts go. Create it through the API (no console step: the
# Slack channel type takes any token that can post, and Monitoring fills the
# team label itself). It posts as the server's own Slack bot, with the token
# from Secret Manager (loop-sessions-slack-bot-token), which must be joined
# to the channel first (conversations.join). Read the token into a file,
# POST {type: slack, labels: {channel_name, auth_token}} to
# projects.notificationChannels.create, and paste the id here. Every
# fleet-tier policy (04-, 04b-, 04c-, 05-, 06a-, 07-, 08-, 09-, 09b-, 02-,
# 15-) renders __SLACK_FLEET__ from it.
SLACK_FLEET=
# The owner's email channel. Create it once, then paste the numeric id:
#   gcloud beta monitoring channels create --type=email \
#     --channel-labels=email_address=you@example.com --display-name=Owner \
#     --format='value(name)'
EMAIL_OWNER=
